---
/**
 * English counterpart of the Spanish context block.
 *
 * The English section of the site mirrors the Spanish one, so the same gap
 * applied: pillar pages sat below the word floor with no explanation of how
 * the deductions actually work. The figures are computed from the same engine,
 * so the two language versions can never contradict each other.
 */
import { calcularSueldoNeto, situacionFamiliarDefecto } from '../lib/irpf-engine';
import { comunidadesAutonomas } from '../data/comunidades-autonomas';

interface Props {
  referencia?: number;
}
const { referencia = 30000 } = Astro.props;

const base = calcularSueldoNeto(referencia, 'MD', situacionFamiliarDefecto, 14);
const mas = calcularSueldoNeto(referencia + 1000, 'MD', situacionFamiliarDefecto, 14);
const marginal = ((1000 - (mas.netoAnual - base.netoAnual)) / 1000) * 100;
const efectiva = (1 - base.netoAnual / referencia) * 100;

const porCcaa = comunidadesAutonomas
  .map((c) => ({ nombre: c.nombre, neto: calcularSueldoNeto(referencia, c.codigo, situacionFamiliarDefecto, 14).netoAnual }))
  .sort((a, b) => b.neto - a.neto);
const mejor = porCcaa[0];
const peor = porCcaa[porCcaa.length - 1];

const eur = (v: number) => '€' + Math.round(v).toLocaleString('en-GB');
const pc = (v: number) => v.toFixed(1) + '%';
---

<section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 mt-12">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-charcoal dark:text-gray-100">How Spanish payroll deductions work</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-dark-gray dark:text-gray-300">
    Two deductions come off a Spanish payslip, and they do not share a base. Social security
    contributions are calculated first, on a contribution base rather than on gross pay directly.
    Income tax withholding follows, applied to gross pay after those contributions. Reversing the
    order overstates the tax, and it is the most common error when the calculation is redone by
    hand.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-dark-gray dark:text-gray-300">
    On {eur(referencia)} gross a year, net pay comes to {eur(base.netoAnual)}, an effective rate of
    {pc(efectiva)}. The next thousand euros, however, is taxed at a marginal rate of
    {pc(marginal)}. The distance between those two figures is what people most often misread,
    because the first describes the whole salary and the second only the next euro earned.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-charcoal dark:text-gray-100">What the region decides</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-dark-gray dark:text-gray-300">
    The state portion of income tax and the social security rates are identical across Spain; the
    whole difference between two regions comes from the autonomous portion. On {eur(referencia)},
    the gap between {mejor.nombre}, which leaves the most, and {peor.nombre}, which leaves the
    least, runs to {eur(mejor.neto - peor.neto)} a year. Real money, though rarely enough on its
    own to justify a move once housing costs are weighed.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-dark-gray dark:text-gray-300">
    The region that applies is the one where you have your habitual tax residence, not where the
    employer is based. A move partway through the year is not apportioned between the two: the tax
    authorities look at where more days were spent, and any correction arrives with the following
    year's return.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-charcoal dark:text-gray-100">Withholding is not the final tax</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-dark-gray dark:text-gray-300">
    What comes off each month is an advance, calculated by the employer from the data it holds:
    expected salary, declared family circumstances and length of service. The final liability is
    settled in the annual return, on the real income of the full year and with deductions applied.
    That is where the June refunds come from, and they are a restitution rather than a bonus.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-dark-gray dark:text-gray-300">
    This is why the modelo 145, the form declaring family circumstances to the employer, is worth
    keeping current. A child born in January, a dependent relative or a recognised disability
    changes the withholding from the moment it is declared and not before. Most large year-end
    adjustments are explained by a form that was never updated.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-charcoal dark:text-gray-100">What a calculation cannot know</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-dark-gray dark:text-gray-300">
    Benefits in kind, from private health cover to a company car, are taxable and raise the base
    without raising the amount paid into the account. Regional deductions vary widely and usually
    have to be claimed rather than applied automatically. Income from outside employment is added
    in the annual return and can move the whole position into a higher band. None of these can be
    inferred from a gross salary.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-dark-gray dark:text-gray-300">
    Withholding tables also assume a stable salary across the year. Anyone changing jobs, earning
    variable pay or holding two employers will see month-to-month differences that are only
    corrected at filing. A gap of a few euros against the payslip is normal rounding; a gap of
    several hundred almost always traces back to one of the points above, and to the payslip where
    it first appeared.
  </p>
</section>
